mathrm{i} at \\lambda =1/100,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}1/20,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}1/10,\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}1/5. Top and bottom rows show Re[u] and Im[u]; shaded backgrounds indicate Re[ϵ] (blue) and Im[ϵ] (pink). Reference transfer-matrix solutions (solid yellow) are compared with Lie-generator (purple dashed) and Helmholtz (green dashed) PINNs.\n\n## Abstract\n\nPhysics-informed neural networks (PINNs) often struggle with one-dimensional wave scattering as the Helmholtz residual pushes the solution toward fast oscillations. This study reformulates the problem through a pair of decoupled first-order equations and learns the ratio of forward and backward wave amplitudes, enforcing transparent boundaries with a hard constraint while recovering phase information by quadrature. For several representative complex permittivity profiles, the resulting “Lie generator” PINN lowers the mean absolute error by up to an order of magnitude and trains about three times faster than a Helmholtz PINN. The focus is on advancing PINNs with stronger physical structure rather than on surpassing classical solvers such as the transfer-matrix method.\n\n## Introduction\n\nWe study one-dimensional wave propagation in lossy media modeled by a complex permittivity – encountered, among many other settings, in laser-plasma problems [1], [2]. \nAccurate Helmholtz solvers underpin applications ranging from integrated photonics to remote sensing, where designers need reliable predictions to screen materials and support control schemes. In such media the field combines fast phase oscillations with damping or gain, so standard discretizations can become stiff and costly. Many large-scale plasma simulations therefore rely on hybrid models that keep the key physics while handing part of the transport to faster solvers [2].\n\nThe Helmholtz equation with transparent boundaries is a common starting point. For layered or piecewise-smooth profiles, the transfer-matrix method (TMM) is a reliable baseline [3]. Accurate treatment of the exterior uses absorbing or non-reflecting boundary conditions [6], or complex absorbing potentials in the Schrödinger analogue [7]. These tools are fast and well known, but their cost can rise with frequency content, and they offer little inductive bias for learning-based surrogates.\n\nWe take a Lie-algebraic view of the first-order system that corresponds to Helmholtz. After a change of variables that separates fast oscillations, the generator is traceless and the propagator lies on the special linear group. Two classic tools support this view. The Wei-Norman factorization expresses the evolution as a product of simple exponentials on fixed generators [22]. The Magnus expansion approximates the logarithm of the propagator by nested integrals while keeping the solution on the group [23], [24]. A related scalar reduction is the variable-phase (Riccati) equation for a reflection-like quantity [25]. This reduction lowers differential order and isolates the most oscillatory part.\n\nPhysics-informed neural networks (PINNs) optimize a solution by minimizing PDE and boundary residuals [26]. Recent theoretical work has established their convergence for broad classes of elliptic and parabolic PDEs, providing formal justification for the method’s consistency [27], [28]. Direct use on Helmholtz in complex media is often stiff despite these guarantees. In that setting, Bayesian hyper-parameter optimization has been shown to substantially improve convergence, underscoring the importance of architecture and training schedules [29]. Complementary advances such as adaptive blending units (ABU) supply trainable activation functions that capture oscillatory patterns more efficiently than fixed bases [30]. We instead learn the Riccati variable and reconstruct amplitudes by quadrature. This keeps the group structure in the loss, reduces stiffness, and preserves flux.\n\nThe paper is organized as follows. Section 2 introduces the theoretical framework and Lie-based reduction. Section 3 details the numerical setup and benchmarks on representative absorbing, reflectionless, and piecewise-linear permittivity profiles. Section 4 concludes with a discussion of implications and future work.\n\n## Problem statement\n\nWe examine one-dimensional monochromatic scattering of a complex-valued field u(z) governed by the Helmholtz-type relation\\frac{{d}^{2}u}{d{z}^{2}}\\left(z\\right)+{k}^{2}\\left(z\\right)u\\left(z\\right)=0,\\phantom{\\rule{2.em}{0ex}}z\\in \\mathcal{Z}:=[0,1],where the refractive profile {k}^{2}\\left(z\\right)\\in \\mathbb{C} may vary across the connected domain. Transparent boundary conditions (TBCs) emulate an unbounded exterior and can be written in the form({\\textstyle \\frac{du}{dz}}+\\mathrm{i}{k}_{-}\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}u){{|}_{z=0}=2\\mathrm{i}{k}_{-}\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}{A}_{\\text{inc}},\\phantom{\\rule{2.em}{0ex}}({\\textstyle \\frac{du}{dz}}-\\mathrm{i}{k}_{+}\\phantom{\\rule{0.16em}{0ex}}u)|}_{z=1}=0,which model a left-incident plane wave A_{inc} without any right-incidence. Here {k}_{-} and {k}_{+} denote the wavenumbers in the left and\n\n## Results and discussion\n\nFig. 1 shows the Gaussian absorbing benchmark with {ϵ}_{0}=0.8+0.08\\mathrm{i} across different wavelengths. The Helmholtz PINN struggles to capture phase and amplitude at small λ values, while the Lie-generator PINN remains aligned with the reference, illustrating the benefit of the reduced formulation in stiff, high-frequency regimes.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTSFC: A Structure-Preserving Form Compiler | SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing | Vol. 40, No. 3 | Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics (https://durham-repository.worktribe.com/OutputFile/1324909)\nciteturn10search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.2 years ago; TSFC: A STRUCTURE-PRESERVING FORM COMPILER ... Helmholtz\nTSFC: A STRUCTURE-PRESERVING FORM COMPILER\nC42